{"message":"Done — international roaming has been enabled for 555-123-2002, and I added 2.0 GB of extra high-speed data.

A $4.00 data refueling charge was added to your bill. Your plan was not changed.

If data doesn’t start working right away, please toggle airplane mode on for about 30 seconds, then turn it off, or restart your phone so it reconnects to a local network in France."}

{"message":"I found the issue on line 555-123-2002: it’s active, but it has used 15.1 GB of a 15.0 GB data limit for the current cycle, so speeds may be slowed or data may stop until the cycle resets on 2025-02-28.

Your plan supports data refueling at $2.00 per GB. To restore high-speed data, I can add more data to this line and the charge will be added to your bill.

How many GB would you like to add?"}

{"message":"International roaming is now enabled for 555-123-2002.

Next single step: turn Airplane Mode on for 30 seconds, then turn it off. This forces your phone to reconnect to a French partner network.

After you do that, tell me whether you see signal bars and LTE/4G/5G."}

{"message":"Good — that means roaming is working, but your phone is connected to a very slow 2G network, which explains the failed or unusable speed test.

Next single step: open your phone’s Cellular/Mobile Network settings and look for a setting called Network Selection or Carrier Selection.

If it is set to Automatic, turn Automatic off so the phone searches for available French networks. Tell me when you see the list of networks, or if you can’t find that setting."}

{"message":"Yes — enable Data Roaming on the phone next.

Roaming is enabled on your account now, but the phone also needs Data Roaming turned on locally to use mobile data abroad.

Please turn on Data Roaming, then wait 1–2 minutes and tell me whether the phone changes from 2G to 4G/LTE/5G or whether it still stays on 2G."}

{"message":"You’re welcome — glad it’s fixed.

Summary of what resolved it: roaming was enabled on the account, Data Roaming was turned on in the phone, and the key issue was the phone being set to “2G only.” Changing it to the 4G/5G preferred option restored 5G service and excellent speed.

You’re all set."}

{"message":"Yes, please run the speed test now.

With Data Roaming now on, 5G signal, mobile data enabled, and the VPN disconnected, it should be able to connect.

After it finishes, send me the download speed, upload speed, and ping/latency — or let me know if it still fails with “No Connection.”"}

{"message":"Yes — just one step for now:

Please turn **Airplane Mode ON**.

Once it is on, tell me, and I’ll give you the next step."}

{"message":"I’m sorry — I know this is upsetting, especially after you already tried the restart and the data refresh. You did everything right.

Let’s keep it to one question at a time:

Is your phone an **iPhone** or an **Android** phone?"}

{"message":"That’s okay — we can figure it out with one simple check.

Please look at the **back of the phone** or the case if it’s visible.

Do you see an **Apple logo** on the back?"}

{"message":"Yes. Let’s do one safe check.

Please open the **Settings** app.

Tell me when Settings is open, and I’ll give you the next step."}

{"message":"Yes — please turn mobile data ON now.

Leave data roaming OFF for the moment unless you’re outside your normal coverage area or traveling internationally. MMS typically needs mobile data, but it should not require roaming in normal coverage.

After turning mobile data on, try sending the picture message once. Let me know what happens — whether it sends, fails, or stays stuck."}

{"message":"Of course — one step at a time.

Step 2 only: Please turn Data Roaming ON now.

After you’ve turned it on, reply and let me know it’s on. Don’t try sending the MMS yet."}

{"message":"Great — thank you. Step 3: Please try sending the MMS/picture message one time now.

Because your phone is still on poor 2G signal, it may take longer than usual or fail, but let’s test it once with both Mobile Data and Data Roaming turned on.

Reply with what happens: sent, failed, or stuck sending."}

{"message":"I understand — that’s frustrating, especially when you’re abroad and just trying to send one picture.

I’ve added 2.0 GB of data to your line, without changing your mobile data plan. The added data charge is $4.00.

Step 4: Please restart your phone now. After it powers back on, let me know what network it shows — for example 2G, 3G, LTE/4G, or 5G. Don’t try the MMS again yet."}

{"message":"Great — that’s exactly what we wanted.

Step 6: Please try sending the MMS/picture message now.

Reply with whether it sends successfully, fails, or stays stuck sending."}

{"message":"Thanks for confirming. Since you’re on 5G with mobile data and roaming on, the next step is to confirm whether mobile data itself is working outside of MMS.

Step 8: With Wi‑Fi still OFF, please open a web browser and try loading a simple website, such as example.com.

Tell me whether the page loads successfully or not."}

{"message":"Yes — please turn Airplane Mode OFF next.

Airplane Mode being on will disable cellular service, which would prevent MMS from sending. After turning it off, wait about 30–60 seconds and check whether cellular bars/service return.

Then tell me whether the SIM still shows as missing."}

{"message":"Yes, please check the SIM status now.

Look for whether it says the SIM/eSIM is active, missing, not detected, or has an error. If you can, also confirm whether Mobile Data is still turned on after Airplane Mode was turned off.

Tell me exactly what the SIM status shows."}

{"message":"Yes — please check the SIM status again now.

I want to confirm whether it still says “No SIM card detected” after the restart, or whether it changed to something like active, searching, no service, or SIM error.

Please tell me the exact SIM status message you see."}

{"message":"Absolutely — let’s do it one step at a time.

Step 1: Please make sure cellular/mobile data is turned ON, then try sending a small MMS while leaving Wi‑Fi as it is for now.

Once you try that, tell me whether the MMS sends or still fails."}

{"message":"Handle the SIM issue first. If the phone says “No cellular service” and “SIM missing,” turning mobile data on won’t help yet because the phone isn’t detecting the SIM/network connection.

Step 1: Please restart the phone once, then check whether the SIM status still says missing.

Don’t change mobile data yet. After the restart, tell me what it shows for cellular service and SIM status."}

{"message":"Absolutely — we’ll do one step at a time.

Step 1: Please turn Wi‑Fi off temporarily, then try sending one MMS while connected only to cellular/mobile data.

After you try it, tell me whether the MMS sends successfully or still fails."}

{"message":"That explains it — Airplane Mode disables cellular service, so MMS cannot send.

Step 1: Turn Airplane Mode off.

After turning it off, wait about 30–60 seconds for your phone to reconnect to a mobile network in France. Then tell me whether you see signal bars or a carrier name in the status bar."}

{"message":"I understand — that’s frustrating. Since it still shows no signal and data disabled, the next single thing to check is whether cellular/mobile data is enabled.

Step 2: Please go to your phone’s cellular/mobile network settings and turn Cellular Data / Mobile Data on.

Don’t change anything else yet. After turning it on, wait about 30 seconds and tell me whether the status bar changes from “data disabled” or still shows no signal."}

{"message":"Thanks for checking. Since you’re abroad, the next single setting to check is Data Roaming.

Step 3: Please turn Data Roaming on for this line.

Don’t change anything else yet. After turning Data Roaming on, wait 30–60 seconds and tell me whether the status bar changes — especially whether signal bars or a carrier name appear, or whether it still says no signal/data disabled."}

{"message":"Thanks — since Airplane Mode is off and both Mobile Data and Data Roaming are on, the next single step is to refresh the phone’s connection.

Step 4: Please restart your phone.

After it powers back on, wait about 60 seconds, then tell me what the status bar shows — specifically whether you see signal bars/carrier name, or if it still says no signal/data disabled."}

{"message":"I’m sorry — I know this is annoying. Since the phone still has no signal after restart, we should avoid changing more settings until I know the exact menu path.

Next step: What kind of phone are you using — iPhone or Android? If Android, please tell me the brand/model if you know it, like Samsung Galaxy or Google Pixel.

Once I know that, I’ll walk you through the next single step to try."}

{
  "message": "Next, please check that Data Roaming is turned ON on the phone itself. Since you’re in France, regular Mobile Data being ON may not be enough — Data Roaming also needs to be enabled locally on the device.",
  "steps": {
    "iphone": "Settings > Cellular > Cellular Data Options > Data Roaming > ON",
    "android": "Settings > Connections or Network & Internet > Mobile Network > Data Roaming > ON"
  },
  "instruction": "Please turn Data Roaming ON, then wait 20–30 seconds and try the speed test again.",
  "note": "Your account-side roaming is already enabled, your line is active, and extra data has been added, so the most likely remaining setting is device-side Data Roaming."
}

{
  "message": "Yes, please turn Data Roaming ON now. After turning it on, wait about 20–30 seconds for the phone to reconnect to the roaming data network, then run the speed test again.",
  "checklist": [
    "Mobile Data ON",
    "Data Roaming ON",
    "VPN disconnected",
    "5G signal showing"
  ]
}

{"message":"Absolutely — do just this one step first:

Turn Airplane Mode on, wait 30 seconds, then turn Airplane Mode off.

That won’t delete or affect your photos. After that, check whether the signal bars come back."}

{"message":"You don’t need to apologize — I understand why that feels stressful. I checked the device information for this line, and the phone is showing as activated, so this does not automatically mean something is wrong with the phone.

Since the line was just reactivated, the next best step is to wait about 5–10 minutes and check again. Sometimes the network takes a little time to fully refresh after a suspension is removed.

Please keep the phone on during that time. If it still says “No Service” after 10 minutes, tell me and I’ll continue helping."}

{"message":"I rechecked the line, and it is still showing Active on our side.

Since you still see “No Service,” please try just one next thing: make sure Cellular/Mobile Data is turned on in your phone settings.

Even though calls and texts should not always depend on data, if the phone shows “data disabled,” turning Cellular/Mobile Data back on may help it reconnect. This will not delete anything on your phone."}

{"message":"No need to guess — we can skip identifying the phone type for now.

Let’s do one simple, safe check next: please try sending a regular text message with no picture to the same person, just something like “test.”

This won’t change any settings. Then tell me whether the regular text sends successfully or also fails."}

{"message":"Thanks for checking. Since it’s still stuck on 2G, the next single step is to check the phone’s preferred network mode and make sure it isn’t limited to 2G.

Please go to your cellular/mobile network settings and set the preferred network type to something like “5G/LTE/3G/2G Auto,” “LTE/3G/2G Auto,” or “Automatic.” Avoid any option that says “2G only” or “GSM only.”

After changing that, wait 1–2 minutes and tell me whether it moves to 3G/4G/LTE/5G."}

{"message":"Yes — no problem. Let’s check safely without changing anything.

Step 1: Look at the back of the phone or the case, if visible. Do you see an Apple logo on the back of the phone?

If you can’t see the back because of a case, don’t remove it unless it’s easy. Just tell me whether you see an Apple logo, or if you’re not sure."}

{"message":"Thanks for trying those steps. Since the phone still says **“No SIM card detected”** after a restart, this is no longer just an MMS/data setting issue — the phone isn’t detecting the SIM/eSIM at all.

I’ve transferred you to a human support agent who can help with SIM/eSIM troubleshooting or reprovisioning while you’re abroad in France.

For context, your account-side changes are already completed: the line is active, 2.0 GB of one-time data was added, and international roaming is enabled."}

{"message":"Absolutely — we’ll do one step at a time.

Step 1: Please restart your phone completely.

Once it has powered back on, wait about 1 minute, then tell me what you see for signal bars and whether mobile data still says disabled."}

{"message":"Done — international roaming is now enabled on 555-123-2002, and I added 2.0 GB of high-speed data.

The data refuel charge is $4.00 total at $2.00/GB and will be added to your bill. No mobile data plan change was made.

If your phone doesn’t reconnect right away, please toggle airplane mode on for about 10 seconds, then turn it off, or restart the phone."}

{"message":"Great — that’s exactly what we wanted to see.

To confirm speed, please open a browser or app and try loading a data-heavy page, such as a news site with images, a maps app, or a short video. If you have a speed test app or website available, you can also run one.

If pages load quickly or video starts without buffering, your high-speed data is working."}

{"message":"Absolutely — let’s do one step at a time.

First, check your phone’s preferred network type so it isn’t locked to 2G.

Please go to your mobile network settings and look for something like “Preferred Network Type,” “Voice & Data,” or “Network Mode.” Tell me what it’s currently set to — for example 2G, 3G, LTE/4G, 5G Auto, or Automatic."}

{"message":"Yes — turn **Data Roaming** on now. Since you’re in France, that setting is required for mobile internet while connected to a partner network abroad.

Roaming has already been enabled on your line, and you added 2 GB of extra data, so your phone should be allowed to use data once that setting is on.

After turning Data Roaming on, wait 30–60 seconds, then try loading a webpage or running the speed test again."}

{"message":"I found the issue on line 555-123-2002: international roaming was disabled, and you’ve also used 15.1 GB out of a 15 GB data limit for the current billing cycle ending 2025-02-28.

I’ve enabled international roaming successfully. Since you’re over your data limit, speeds may still be reduced unless we add more data. Your plan’s data refuel rate is $2.00 per GB.

How many GB would you like to add to restore better data performance?"}

{"message":"Try a simple webpage first — it’s a lighter test than a speed test and works better when the connection may be weak.

Please open a basic site like example.com or your carrier account page. Wait up to 30 seconds.

Then tell me whether it loads, partially loads, or still says no connection."}

{"message":"Yes — change it to 4G/5G preferred, or the closest option like 5G/LTE/3G/2G Auto or LTE/3G/2G Auto.

After changing it, wait about 60 seconds for the phone to reconnect. Then check the status bar and run the speed test again if it shows LTE/4G/5G.

Tell me what network indicator you see afterward and the new speed result if you can test it."}

{"message":"Excellent — I’m glad that fixed it.

To recap: your account roaming was enabled and the 2 GB data refuel was applied; the remaining issue was the phone being set to 2G only. Switching it to 4G/5G preferred restored 5G and full-speed data.

You should be all set in France now. Safe travels!"}

{"message":"That would explain the “No Service.” When the SIM is locked with a PIN, the phone can’t connect to the mobile network until it’s unlocked.

Please unlock the SIM by entering your SIM PIN when prompted. If you don’t know the SIM PIN, don’t keep guessing—too many wrong attempts can lock it further and require a PUK code.

Do you know the SIM PIN, or would you like me to transfer you to a human agent to help with the SIM/PUK unlock process?"}

{
  "response": "Absolutely — I understand. Since you’re feeling overwhelmed and we’ve already confirmed the line is active, added data, and restarted the phone, I’ve transferred you to a human support agent who can walk you through this very slowly and safely.\n\nThey’ll already know what we tried, including that your photos are important and you don’t want to change risky settings."
}

{"message":"Absolutely — let’s do it one step at a time.

Step 1: Please turn Wi‑Fi off, then confirm that mobile/cellular data is turned on. After that, try sending a small MMS again.

Let me know what happens."}

{"message":"Thanks for checking. Since mobile data and data roaming are both on but you’re still stuck on poor 2G, the next single step is to refresh the phone’s network connection.

Please turn Airplane Mode on, wait 30 seconds, then turn Airplane Mode off.

After it reconnects, tell me what the network indicator shows — for example 2G, 3G, 4G, LTE, or 5G — and whether the signal is still poor."}

{"message":"Thanks. Since the network refresh didn’t improve it, the next single step is to restart the phone.

Please power the phone off, wait about 30 seconds, then power it back on.

After it reconnects, tell me what the status bar shows for signal and network type."}

{"message":"I understand — thanks for sticking with it. Since mobile data and roaming are on, and a restart didn’t improve the poor 2G connection, the next step is to check the phone’s network selection.

Please open your cellular/mobile network settings and check whether Network Selection is set to Automatic.

For now, just check that setting and tell me whether it’s Automatic or Manual."}

{"message":"Choose “4G/5G preferred.”

That gives the phone the best chance to connect to a usable data network while still allowing it to pick between 4G and 5G as available.

After selecting it, wait 30–60 seconds and tell me what the status bar shows."}

{"message":"Yes. Now that the SIM is detected and you have cellular signal, turn Mobile Data/Cellular Data on.

Because you’re in France, also turn Data Roaming on if it isn’t already enabled in the phone settings. Your account-side roaming is already enabled for this line.

After turning those on, wait 1–2 minutes for the phone to register on the roaming data network, then try sending the MMS again. If it stays on 2G or MMS still fails, let me know what the status bar shows after mobile data/roaming are enabled."}

{"message":"Yes. The next step is to reseat the SIM if your phone uses a physical SIM.

Please power the phone off, remove the SIM card, reinsert it securely, then power the phone back on. After it restarts, check whether it still says “no SIM card detected.”

If your phone does not have a physical SIM tray or you’re using eSIM only, tell me that instead — in that case, this likely needs a carrier-side eSIM/SIM reactivation check."}

{"message":"Great — the phone is now detecting the SIM and mobile data is enabled.

Next single step: try sending the MMS again while mobile data is on.

If it fails, tell me whether you see any specific error message. Since the phone is only showing poor 2G signal, the MMS may still fail if the cellular connection is too weak."}

{"message":"Yes. Please do just this one step:

Move near a window or outside, then wait about 1–2 minutes.

After that, check the status bar and tell me what signal/network type it shows, such as 2G, LTE, 4G, or 5G."}

{"message":"Since it’s still stuck on poor 2G, the next single step is to check the phone’s network mode.

Please go to Cellular/Mobile Network settings and look for “Preferred network type” or “Network mode.” Make sure it is set to an automatic option like LTE/4G/5G Auto — not “2G only.”

After checking, tell me what network mode it’s currently set to."}
